{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '550,775p'\nrg -n ':global|blocked|blocking|unbound|nil|as-is|root.scop|producer|print.var|bound\\\\?' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/datom.world.md\nnl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md | sed -n '550,775p'\nrg -n ':global|blocked|blocking|unbound|nil|as-is|root.scop|producer|print.var|bound\\\\?' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, fundamental primitives (tuples, streams), design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams. Persistent facts are immutable tuples and datoms `[e a v t m]`.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is events on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata).\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly. Streams carry whatever values the consumer needs.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing — the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how the second\naxiom becomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in Yin.VM that communicate with the outside world through DaoStream. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/agent.tzu.md`](./agent.tzu.md) and [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for agent and VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nHost-specific boundaries are isolated with DaoStream and interpreters of\nDaoStream. A host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call.\n\nDaoStream handle operations are the one sanctioned function-shaped contact\nwith that boundary: their exhaustive outcome maps are stream protocol, not an\napplication callback. Above those operations, portable code still expresses\nhost work as effect values and observes outcomes as stream data.\n\nPortable code appends an effect value describing what must happen. A host\ninterpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and appends the\noutcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by identity in the\ndata; neither side holds a reference to the other.\n\nAn adapter that exposes a function for portable code to call is not an\ninterpreter. It isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the\neffect never appears as an emission.\n\nA callback is an event on a stream. That is the same rule in the other\ndirection: when the host initiates -- a socket receives, a connection drops, a\ntimer fires -- the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\n\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**An adapter is a map of those functions. That is all an adapter is.** It is a\nvalue, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files, timers and windows\nall deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is what they call. The\nhost library is handed the map. Each entry takes the host's arguments and\nreturns one value; the append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry\ncannot return a value to the host and cannot invoke application code. The\nadapter is handed only the deposit operation and its own host resource. If its\ndeposit channel fails, it may close that resource to make the boundary\nobservably gone; this is teardown, not an upward callback. Host types are\nclassified into plain data here -- a transform is the last place holding a host\nerror object, and no host type may cross onto the stream.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read, and it does\nnot need to. This is what keeps the layering one-way: a boundary depends on\nnothing above it, because whoever constructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of those events semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol\nvalidation happen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport\nmachinery, not an event the adapter is obliged to deposit. Once the transport\nhas produced a valid host event, data is syntax (axiom 2), so worth is not a\nproperty of the datum -- an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to\nanother that does not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys\nthe perspectives that have not been taken. How much is retained is the medium's\nretention policy, which is operational; what an event means is interpretation.\nNeither is the depositor's decision.\n\nAn adapter that takes a function to invoke has relocated the callback, not\nremoved it, even when every value it passes is plain data. An adapter whose\ncallback has a meaningful return value is worse: that is a synchronous call in\nboth directions wearing the shape of an event.\n\nHost events are ordinary values on an ordinary stream. A transport may use a\nqualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and additive evolution;\nthat envelope remains plain data and creates no separate event mechanism. A\ncomposition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary for facts that it\nowns. Neither requires a bespoke bus or callback API.\n\nHost error types never cross the boundary. A transform function classifies them\nand emits a qualified value.\n\nA host with no implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported\nresult. That is a correct outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a new tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical, content-hashed tuple structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema,\na row shape), check whether the fact it would encode is instead computable\nfrom the existing structure via a Datalog query over `dao.space.query/q`,\nat the correct scope for what the fact actually depends on — not every\nquery over the shallowest available relation is sound; get the scope\nwrong and a query can silently mis-derive the fact instead of failing\nloudly. If a correctly-scoped query can derive it, it belongs as a query\nor a projection, not as new persisted structure: new structure is\ncontent-hashed and migrates every downstream consumer, and duplicates\nauthority over a fact a query could derive fresh instead. This is not a\nzero-cost trade — deriving still costs real query complexity and, if the\nfact isn't already indexed, the work of building and maintaining whatever\ndoes index it — but that cost is paid once, in the query and its index,\nnot in every future migration of the persisted structure. See\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md)'s\nretired `:global` tag for the concrete case study — a free/bound variable\ndistinction that looked like it needed a new AST node, until it turned\nout to be computable by query; getting the scope right there took two\nrounds and a real correctness bug (a query that looked sufficient\nundercounted a dependency in a case the simpler query didn't cover).\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\nFor advanced topics (parallel transport, capability tokens, entanglement):\nSee [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md)\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/file-format.md`](../agents/file-format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n   550\t`(add 2 1)` address differently, because `order-normalize` sorts maps and\n   551\tsets but leaves vectors in place (`jing.cljc:45-64`). Literal maps and sets\n   552\tinside a `data` slot hash order-insensitively, which is correct: they are\n   553\tvalues.\n   554\t\n   555\t### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited; ordinary identity use is unblocked\n   556\t\n   557\t`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized, metadata-aware hand printer\n   558\t(`jing.cljc`'s `order-normalize` / `canonical-print`). The three\n   559\tconformance pairs this section used to name as blocking collisions are\n   560\tnow closed:\n   561\t\n   562\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   563\t| Two different values                                   | Address today                       | Why it matters                                                           |\n   564\t+========================================================+====================================+==========================================================================+\n   565\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]`              | distinct                            | `conj` appends to one and prepends to the other                          |\n   566\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   567\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (seq [1 2])]`        | distinct                            | a seq is sequential but not a list, and both differ from the vector      |\n   568\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   569\t| `[:literal ^{:a 1} [1 2]]` and `[:literal ^{:a 2} [1   | distinct                            | `data` admits plain metadata (`linearize.cljc:58`) and programs may read |\n   570\t| 2]]`                                                   |                                      | it; collection metadata is now address-significant                      |\n   571\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   572\t\n   573\t`dao.jing.md`'s Canonical encoding section records the encoder's current\n   574\tcontract precisely and its remaining residuals: scalar (symbol) metadata\n   575\tis not address-significant, pathological symbols whose print mimics\n   576\tanother value's can still collide, byte arrays are hashed by identity\n   577\trather than content, and ambient print-var bindings still reach scalar\n   578\tbytes. None of the three pairs above are affected by any of those\n   579\tresiduals — they were the structural (collection-level) gaps this design\n   580\tdepended on closing, and they are closed. **The identity and\n   581\tdeduplication block this section used to impose is lifted for ordinary\n   582\tvalues** — rows, vectors, ledger-record maps, flat-projection row\n   583\tcollapse (§6.1), DAG sharing (§4.4), process-local address-keyed caches,\n   584\tand `dao.jing` reads that resolve an address to a literal value may treat\n   585\ttwo equal addresses as one value. The remaining residuals above are\n   586\tpre-existing, scoped, and tracked as `dao.jing.md` Open Items rather than\n   587\tas a design-wide block, but they are not uniformly latent: scalar\n   588\tmetadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to emit\n   589\tthose specific value shapes before they matter, while ambient print-var\n   590\tbindings (`*print-readably*` and similar) reach scalar bytes for ANY\n   591\tscalar today — that residual is live now for any caller that hashes\n   592\tinside such a binding, not conditional on a future producer.\n   593\t\n   594\tThe pinned canonical byte encoding itself — the target `dao.jing.md`\n   595\tdescribes as its first open item, replacing this transitional hand\n   596\tprinter — remains open work; when it lands, every address minted under\n   597\tthis design changes with every other `dao.jing` address, the dependency\n   598\tUCF §7.3.2 already accepts.\n   599\t\n   600\t### 4.3 Every node is addressed by construction\n   601\t\n   602\tPer-node addressing is not a projection consequence and no longer a\n   603\tfollow-up: a row's id is its node's address by construction (§2.1), so\n   604\tevery node of every tree is named, shareable, and queryable the moment it\n   605\tis projected. The round-7 question — a `:lambda` whose body is\n   606\t*materialized separately*, referred to by address from the canonical form\n   607\tof the enclosing tree, pinned by manifests on its own — is the ordinary\n   608\tshape of the stored row DAG, not a canonical-form extension: every child\n   609\tslot already refers by address, and a manifest may name any row's id. What\n   610\tremains of it is a lowering question — one segment per lambda rather than\n   611\tout-of-line bodies in one segment (`linearize.cljc:151-158`) — which is a\n   612\t§5.2.1 profile concern, changes no row address, and is not open here.\n   613\t\n   614\t### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\n   615\t\n   616\tToday a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned\n   617\tentity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda\n   618\t(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it\n   619\t(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each\n   620\tcall-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\n   621\t\n   622\tContent addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one\n   623\t`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\n   624\taddress by construction and one row in the stored set, and a query \"which\n   625\tsites reference this lambda's content\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\n   626\tBecause this design retains named variables in the canonical tuple (§2, §5.1),\n   627\tthis structural sharing requires exact identity, not alpha-equivalence:\n   628\ttwo functions that are identical up to variable renaming project to\n   629\t*different* rows, since the parameter and variable names are part of the\n   630\thashed content. Only subtrees identical in structure *and* in every retained\n   631\tname collapse to one row. The row set holds each such distinct subtree once,\n   632\tand parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\n   633\t**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective over ordinary values, which it now is.** The three conformance pairs (list/vector, seq/list, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal. The disclosed residuals (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte-array identity hashing) remain gaps in the transitional encoder, but none of them are reachable through this collapse for ordinary values — see §4.2.\n   634\t`:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the codec.\n   635\t\n   636\tWhat `:eid` also did, and content addresses do not, is name a *place*.\n   637\t\"This call site\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\n   638\tnot the site's content address; a query that needs distinct call sites\n   639\tjoins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\n   640\tlink that must survive a second identical site, a second identical file,\n   641\tor a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\n   642\taddresses serve sharing and cross-media correspondence of content;\n   643\toccurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\n   644\t\n   645\t### 4.5 Free variables are queried, not tagged\n   646\t\n   647\tAn earlier revision of this design split variable references into two\n   648\tnode types: `:variable` for names bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s\n   649\tparams, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n   650\tfree names, resolved through `resolve-var`'s store → primitives →\n   651\tmodules fallthrough. The motivation was static inspectability: knowing a\n   652\tprogram's free-name dependencies (for UCF §7.6.1's `:yin.k/requires`\n   653\tfixed point, and for capability checking before running untrusted\n   654\tcontent) without evaluating it.\n   655\t\n   656\tThat motivation does not require a second tag. Whether a `:variable` row\n   657\tis free or bound is exactly what a **query** over `$ast` (§6.1) can\n   658\tcompute: walk the row's ancestor chain (a `node`/`nodes` slot pointing at\n   659\ta row's own id is a parent-child edge, so the ancestor relation is a\n   660\trecursive Datalog rule over the row relation itself, needing no separate\n   661\toccurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\n   662\tin any enclosing `:lambda`'s `params`. If it does not, the name is free —\n   663\ta `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n   664\talready supports recursive rules (`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`'s\n   665\t`eval-rule`), so this is not a capability gap; it is a query the design\n   666\tnever needed to avoid.\n   667\t\n   668\tSplitting the tag instead of querying for the same fact bought nothing\n   669\tthe query could not already give, and cost real things: the name\n   670\tcollided with the project's own \"no hidden global state\" invariant — the\n   671\t`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n   672\ttime-varying (it holds `yin/def` results alongside stream handles and\n   673\tcursor entries, §7.7.2), so the naming confusion was a real signal, not\n   674\tcosmetic; the no-fallthrough contract (`:variable`'s lexical-only\n   675\tresolution, evaluating an unbound name to `nil` rather than falling\n   676\tthrough to store/primitives/modules) replaced a forgiving runtime\n   677\tfallthrough with a requirement that every frontend get free/bound\n   678\tclassification exactly right, which is precisely the hardest part of\n   679\twriting a correctly hygienic macro expander; and the change depended on\n   680\tfrontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n   681\tauthoring time) that does not exist anywhere in this codebase. This is\n   682\tnow a decided case study for the broader principle in\n   683\t[`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md)'s Design Principles:\n   684\tbefore adding structure to a canonical, content-hashed representation,\n   685\tcheck whether a query already gives you the fact for free.\n   686\t\n   687\tSee `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc` for a working demonstration: a\n   688\trecursive rule (`edge`/`anc`/`depth`/`bound?`, verified against the real\n   689\t`dao.space.query/q` engine, not sketched) computing the free-name set of\n   690\ta row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n   691\tnearest-enclosing-binder case under shadowing. `edge`'s clauses are\n   692\thardcoded for the fixture's two tags (`:lambda`'s body slot,\n   693\t`:application`'s operator/operands slots), not generic over §2.3's full\n   694\tgrammar — a tree using a tag with other node-valued slots (`:if`,\n   695\t`:dao.stream.apply/call`, `:vm/resume`, the `:stream/*` family) has\n   696\tedges this specific rule does not walk, so it can call a bound name free\n   697\t(an overcount, not an undercount — still conservative-safe for §7.6.1,\n   698\tjust imprecise). A production rule needs an `edge` clause per node-valued\n   699\tslot in the grammar, or a schema-driven walk over\n   700\t`semantic-bytecode-grammar` instead of one clause per tag.\n   701\t\n   702\t**The row-only query is unsound, not just imprecise, when a name is free\n   703\tat one occurrence and bound at another — and this is not a permanent\n   704\tcost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n   705\tstructurally identical subtrees share one row (§4.4), a name that is\n   706\tfree at one occurrence and bound at another (e.g. `((fn [x] x) x)`, whose\n   707\toperand `x` is free while the lambda body's `x` is bound) collapses to\n   708\tone `:variable` row with multiple parent edges — one through the binding\n   709\tlambda, one not. `bound?`'s row-level walk (`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`)\n   710\tfinds a binding ancestor via *either* edge and calls the whole row bound,\n   711\twhich would silently drop the genuinely free occurrence from a computed\n   712\t`:yin.k/requires` set. UCF §7.6.1 requires that set to be a **conservative**\n   713\tfixed point — it must never undercount what a program depends on, since\n   714\tundercounting a capability requirement is a security defect, not an\n   715\timprecision. The row-only query can undercount in this mixed case, so it\n   716\tis not sufficient for `:yin.k/requires` on its own.\n   717\t\n   718\tThe fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n   719\t*specific occurrence* is exactly a places-not-contents question, and\n   720\t§6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\n   721\t(`[origin root-address path ...]`, §2.5) rather than row identity: walk\n   722\teach occurrence's own path to its ancestors, not the row's abstracted\n   723\tparent edges. This is not merely argued — `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`'s\n   724\t`occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\n   725\tagainst the real `q` engine, on the exact `((fn [x] x) x)` case above: a\n   726\thand-built occurrence relation of §2.5's shape (the production indexer\n   727\tthat would emit it does not exist yet, so the test builds it as fixture\n   728\tdata, same honest scoping as the row-level demo), a recursive rule\n   729\twalking path-prefix ancestry rather than row edges, and — the point of\n   730\tthe exercise — a **contrast assertion that runs the row-only rule against\n   731\tthe same database and confirms it actually does undercount** (`#{}`\n   732\tinstead of `#{'x}`), so the failure mode §4.5 describes above is\n   733\tdemonstrated, not narrated. No new structure was needed; only the\n   734\tricher, occurrence-joined query, exactly as this section claims.\n   735\t\n   736\t**Not root-scoped as written — a real gap, not a hedge.** The test's\n   737\t`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?` rules take only `?path` and `?name` as arguments;\n   738\tthey never thread a root through the recursion. Over a single tree this\n   739\tis harmless, but a production occurrence relation holds every reachable\n   740\ttree's occurrences together, and structural paths are not\n   741\troot-qualified — two different trees can produce the identical literal\n   742\tpath (e.g. both have a lambda at path `[2]`). `occ-anc`/`occ-bound?` as\n   743\twritten would then find a binder in the WRONG tree and misclassify an\n   744\toccurrence in one tree against a binder in another. Whoever wires up the\n   745\treal `:yin.k/requires` computation (open item 5, \"Conservative dependency\n   746\tcompletion\") must thread `?root` through every rule head\n   747\t(`p-up`/`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?`) and constrain the `[$occ ...]` join to\n   748\tone root-address, not lift the test's rule set unmodified. What the test\n   749\tdoes prove, correctly: the occurrence-joined technique itself is sound\n   750\tand outperforms the row-only rule even across the whole §2.3 grammar\n   751\t(the occurrence rule walks path prefixes generically, so it does not\n   752\tshare the row-only rule's separate gap of only knowing `:lambda`'s and\n   753\t`:application`'s node-valued slots — see the row-only rule's own\n   754\tlimitation noted below). What remains before production use is\n   755\troot-scoping the rules and building the indexer that emits occurrence\n   756\ttuples in the first place — both implementation work, not open design\n   757\tquestions.\n   758\t\n   759\t---\n   760\t\n   761\t## §5 The segment level\n   762\t\n   763\t### 5.1 The instruction vector is UCF §7.3.2\n   764\t\n   765\tThe segment's canonical form is the positional instruction tuple vector of\n   766\tUCF §7.3.2: one tuple per instruction, pc is the index, the\n   767\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 table fixes each mnemonic's arity and operand\n   768\tkinds, defaults are saturated, refs are resolved pcs, and the header folds\n   769\taway. Its address is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`, the value\n   770\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.2 already types `:yin.code/hash` as. The two\n   771\tlevels are uniform under this design: an AST row (§2.1) and an instruction\n   772\ttuple are both flat positional tuples, canonical and content-addressed,\n   773\tand the instruction vector was already this shape before the flat-row\n   774\truling clarified the AST level. To perfectly preserve program semantics across boundaries, both the Universal Map AST and the Semantic Tuples strictly retain named variables. Therefore, `\"v2\"` everywhere in this document means the Named Variable grammar. This design otherwise adds\n   775\tnothing to that form and restates none of it.\n156:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\n228:|         | nil, booleans, numbers, strings, |                                                                                                                           |\n251:| `bool`  | exactly `true` or `false`        | never nil, never absent                                                                                                   |\n337:and a row with a nil in a saturated slot is invalid, not defaulted.\n555:### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited; ordinary identity use is unblocked\n559:conformance pairs this section used to name as blocking collisions are\n577:rather than content, and ambient print-var bindings still reach scalar\n588:metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to emit\n589:those specific value shapes before they matter, while ambient print-var\n592:inside such a binding, not conditional on a future producer.\n649:params, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n663:a `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n671:`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n675:resolution, evaluating an unbound name to `nil` rather than falling\n680:frontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n688:recursive rule (`edge`/`anc`/`depth`/`bound?`, verified against the real\n690:a row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n704:cost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n709:lambda, one not. `bound?`'s row-level walk (`test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`)\n718:The fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n736:**Not root-scoped as written — a real gap, not a hedge.** The test's\n737:`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?` rules take only `?path` and `?name` as arguments;\n742:path (e.g. both have a lambda at path `[2]`). `occ-anc`/`occ-bound?` as\n747:(`p-up`/`occ-anc`/`occ-bound?`) and constrain the `[$occ ...]` join to\n755:root-scoping the rules and building the indexer that emits occurrence\n910:for ordinary values it is no longer blocked (§4.2). The occurrence\n968:  `slots-match?` (`query.cljc:444-449`) treats only `_`, nil, and the\n1008:| attribute-dimension queries with the attribute unbound, `[?e ?a ?v]` | a flat row has no attribute slot; \"every fact about this node\" is an EAV question               |\n1094:mirror `code/well-formed?` (`code.cljc:155-180`): each returns nil or the\n1114:| `:saturation`     | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil                                                                                                                    |\n1132:restricted admission domain, and ordinary identity uses are unblocked.\n1154:| `:saturation`    | a saturated operand (`:gensym` prefix, `:stream-make` buffer, `:call` tail?, `:ffi-call` argc) is nil                                               |\n1163:There is no vector-level binding rule for `:var`: nesting is a property of the AST, checked by §7.4; an unbound `:var` name at run time evaluates to `nil` (§7.7.2).\n1212: :where [$occ ?root ?path ?v] [?v :variable ?name] (not (bound? ?root ?path ?name))]\n1242:[:find ?name :in $code % :where [$code _ _ :var ?name] (not (bound? ?name))]\n1345:**Under-arity calls leave missing parameters bound to `nil`, not unbound.**\n1346:An under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an\n1351:absent from the extended environment rather than explicitly `nil` — so it\n1354:under that name. After the fix, a missing parameter is `nil` and shadows\n1410:footprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\n1460:local integer or tempid, `t` nil at write time, and `m` a reserved marker.\n1754:  new producer, not a narrowing of the legacy one, and the two are kept\n1897:| walker, `ast_walker.cljc`                        | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. `:variable` arm (`:361-363`) is unchanged — still resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (§4.5). Closure application (`:191`, hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`) changes from `(zipmap params args)` to nil-filling missing params. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2).                                                                                                      |\n1912:| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; frame binding for `:closure` matches arguments to `params` by position, binding each to its name (§7.7.2); apply (`semantic.cljc:200`) changes from `zipmap` to nil-filling missing params; `:var` opcode is unchanged, resolving env → store → primitives → modules (§4.5); decodes the new operand (`:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n1985:   inherited, still open, but **no longer blocking ordinary identity and\n1987:   this item used to name as blocking (vector/list, vector/seq,\n1993:   ambient print-var bindings on scalars — `dao.jing.md`'s Open Items)\n2082:    the argument-to-named-parameter binding rule of §7.7.2 (arguments match `params` by position, each bound to its name; under-arity call → missing parameter names bound to `nil`; extra arguments beyond the params list length are dropped)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
