{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\").read_text().splitlines()\nfor a,b in [(233,304),(776,1250)]:\n for i in range(a-1,b):\n  x=s[i]\n  if x.startswith(\\\"+\\\"):continue\n  print(f\\\"{i+1}: \\\"+(\\\" \\\".join(x.split()) if x.startswith(\\\"|\\\") else x))'\"","aggregated_output":"233: | `key` | a store key: any value of the | the store contract is a map lookup with no key restriction (`ast_walker.cljc:401`, `:402-404`); the linearizer admits any |\n234: | | `data` kind | `plain-data?` key (`linearize.cljc:79-85`, `:138-141`) and the loader carries it through unchanged |\n235: | | | (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:577-579`), so symbols (`yin/def`), keywords (`test/yin/vm/parity_test.cljc:99`), and|\n236: | | | numbers are all portable keys today. `key` is a named kind rather than `data` only so that the same definition is cited by|\n237: | | | AST validation (§7.4), instruction validation (§7.5), dependency extraction (§7.7), and the store-slice encoding of UCF |\n238: | | | §7.6.2; it is the `data` domain and nothing narrower |\n240: | `sym` | one symbol | |\n242: | `syms` | a vector of symbols, | one slot, so arity stays fixed however many parameters there are; the |\n243: | | possibly empty | ordered symbols in a row, the symbols in order in the semantic layer |\n245: | `kw` | one keyword | |\n247: | `str` | one string | |\n249: | `int` | one non-negative integer | |\n251: | `bool` | exactly `true` or `false` | never nil, never absent |\n253: \n254: ### 2.3 The table\n255: \n256: The tag set is the walker's dispatch set reconciled with the codec's\n257: inventory (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:413-478`) under the two boundary calls\n258: of §3. The `Body arity` column counts the body items (tag plus slots). A full row's count is its body arity plus one (for the ID). This table is the **projection and reconstruction\n259: dictionary**: the slot list of a tag, in order, is exactly the map's key\n260: set beyond `:type` — the arity ↔ key-set correspondence — so the one\n261: table fixes the row's positions and the map's fields together. Position\n262: `i` of a row body holds the value of the field the *Slots* column names\n263: at `i`; projection reads the positions, reconstruction reads the names,\n264: and neither side may disagree with the other.\n265: \n267: | Tag | Body arity | Row body | Slots | Slot kinds | Walker arm | Codec arm |\n269: | :literal | 2 | [:literal value] | value | data | ast_walker.cljc:360| v2.cljc:414-415 |\n271: | :variable | 2 | [:variable name] | name | sym | unchanged: `ast_walker.cljc:361-363` | unchanged: `v2.cljc:417-418` |\n273: | :lambda | 3 | [:lambda params body] | params body | syms, node | unchanged: `ast_walker.cljc:364-370` | unchanged: `v2.cljc:419-424` |\n275: | :application | 4 | [:application operator operands | operator operands tail? | node, nodes, bool| :371-376 | :424-429 |\n276: | | | tail?] | | | | |\n278: | :if | 4 | [:if test consequent alternate] | test consequent alternate| node, node, node | :377-381 | :435-441 |\n280: | :dao.stream.apply/call | 3 | [:dao.stream.apply/call op operands] | op operands | kw, nodes | :382-394 | :430-434 |\n282: | :vm/gensym | 2 | [:vm/gensym prefix] | prefix | str | :395-400 | :443-444 |\n284: | :vm/store-get | 2 | [:vm/store-get key] | key | key | :401 | :445-446 |\n286: | :vm/store-put | 3 | [:vm/store-put key val] | key val | key, data | :402-409 | :447-449 |\n288: | :vm/current-continuation| 1 | [:vm/current-continuation] | — | — | :422-427 | :474-475 |\n290: | :vm/park | 1 | [:vm/park] | — | — | :428-430 | :469 |\n292: | :vm/resume | 3 | [:vm/resume parked-id val] | parked-id val | kw, node | :431-438 | :470-473 |\n294: | :stream/make | 2 | [:stream/make buffer] | buffer | int | :439-447 | :451-453 |\n296: | :stream/put | 3 | [:stream/put target val] | target val | node, node | :448-454 | :454-458 |\n298: | :stream/cursor | 2 | [:stream/cursor source] | source | node | :455-461 | :459-461 |\n300: | :stream/next | 2 | [:stream/next source] | source | node | :462-468 | :462-464 |\n302: | :stream/close | 2 | [:stream/close source] | source | node | added by §3.2 | :465-467 |\n304: \n776: \n777: ### 5.2 The hash chain is realized by derivation records\n778: \n779: ```\n780: tree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\n781: ```\n782: \n783: Neither value carries the other's address: the tree is the input, the\n784: vector excludes `:yin.code/derived-from` (UCF §7.3.2, *The header folds\n785: away*). The link is a **derivation record**, itself content-addressed\n786: (§8.2):\n787: \n788: ```clojure\n789: {:yin.ledger/op       :derive\n790:  :yin.ledger/input    <tree-address>\n791:  :yin.ledger/output   <segment-address>\n792:  :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\n793:  :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-v1\"          ; the lowering profile, §5.2.1\n794:                        :yin.code/contract \"v2\"              ; the UCF §7.3.3 execution contract it targets\n795:                        :yin.k/version     0}}\n796: ```\n797: \n798: Its address is published in the ledger (§8.3). The chain\n799: `record → input, record → output` is what \"the hash chain is the ledger\"\n800: means under this design: the chain lives in ledger records and refs, not\n801: in the code content, which rightly excludes it.\n802: \n803: #### 5.2.1 The lowering profile\n804: \n805: UCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\n806: the transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.\n807: Its declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\n808: two deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\n809: different, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable\n810: only against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:\n811: \n813: | Pinned by the profile | Reference value for `\"ast-v1\"` |\n815: | input grammar | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document |\n817: | normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else |\n819: | emission rules | the §5.3 flattening table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right) |\n821: | label and body order | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were |\n822: | | encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`) |\n824: | terminators | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`) |\n826: | target execution contract | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy |\n828: \n829: The profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution\n830: contract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\n831: profile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the\n832: execution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\n833: choice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured\n834: map above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection\n835: (§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.\n836: \n837: #### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps\n838: \n839: A consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two\n840: different things and reports them separately:\n841: \n842: 1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\n843:    `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the value. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and\n844:    says nothing about the derivation.\n845: 2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's\n846:    `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it lowers the reconstructed map under that profile and verifies the resulting instruction-vector address against `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality\n847:    verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-\n848:    mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A\n849:    consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports\n850:    `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` naming both profiles, the same outcome UCF\n851:    §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\n852:    output vector if the vector's own execution contract is one it\n853:    implements, because content integrity (step 1) is independent of who\n854:    lowered it.\n855: \n856: Two trees that lower to one vector under one profile yield two records\n857: with one output; one tree lowered under two profiles yields two records\n858: with two outputs, and neither is corruption.\n859: \n860: ### 5.3 Instruction provenance\n861: \n862: `:yin.code/source` (`v2.cljc:326`, `linearize.cljc:225`) leaves the\n863: instruction: UCF §7.3.2 excludes it from the vector, and the tuple form has\n864: nowhere else to put it. It becomes the side table `[segment-address pc\n865: origin root-address path]` of §2.5, emitted by the lowering beside the\n866: vector and keyed by the full occurrence key, so that two identical call\n867: sites, two identical files, two identical trees at two batch indices, or\n868: two expansion attempts map to distinct instruction ranges. The `origin`,\n869: including the member index `j` for admitted syntax, is an input to the\n870: lowering, supplied by the composition that admitted or generated the\n871: tree; the lowering never infers it. Rendering an instruction range back to\n872: source is a join over that table and the source-position table on the\n873: occurrence key, which is the query `yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.3 says it is;\n874: for generated code the join goes through the producing event's record\n875: address to its call occurrence (§8.4).\n876: \n877: ---\n878: \n879: ## §6 Querying\n880: \n881: ### 6.1 The row relation and the two directions around it\n882: \n883: The flat rows are the canonical stored form (§2.1), and `q` runs over\n884: them directly — ruling 3 of 2026-09-14 was verified live over exactly\n885: this shape. A stored set holds one row per distinct subtree: identical\n886: subtrees project to the identical row, so the set is the tree's DAG\n887: (§4.4). Because a tag has one arity in the tree, it has one arity in the\n888: relation (tag arity plus one for the id). Rows of one tag are\n889: homogeneous, so a where-pattern for a tag has exactly that tag's arity\n890: and no padding. Rows from many trees union into one relation without\n891: collision, because ids are addresses. An occurrence count is a count\n892: over parent slots or over the occurrence relation below, never over\n893: rows.\n894: \n895: **The Dedicated AST Indexer:** This row relation (called `$ast` in queries) is maintained by the dedicated AST indexer (§1). The indexer is a `dao.stream` observer peer to the evaluators (§7.1); its input is the row batches of §7.1; its output is this row relation plus the occurrence relation below; it is distinct from `dao.space.index`, which indexes datoms only (§6.5); it is a projection keeper per §1's Query layer, so discarding it loses nothing.\n896: \n897: `map → rows` is the **codec boundary projection** (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`): strip the §2.5\n898: exclusions, saturate per §2.4, positionalize per the §2.3 dictionary,\n899: merkle per §4.1. It is the standing contract of the Encoder Observer, not a\n900: migration device (§9.1).\n901: \n902: `rows → map` is **load-time reconstruction** (§7.1): validate the rows\n903: (§7.4), then rebuild the map AST through the ids. The precedent is\n904: `datoms->ast` (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:494-559`), which already\n905: rebuilds map ASTs from flat rows through entity ids — a `get-attr` per\n906: field over an entity index, recursion through child ids; the loader is\n907: its successor with content addresses in place of allocated ids.\n908: \n909: Deduplication — treating two equal ids as one value — is an identity use;\n910: for ordinary values it is no longer blocked (§4.2). The occurrence\n911: relation is still what places, not contents, questions go through —\n912: that requirement was never about the encoder block, and it stands\n913: regardless (§4.5's mixed-occurrence case is exactly this: id equality is\n914: sound, but a places question still needs the occurrence relation, not id\n915: equality alone).\n916: \n917: A second relation, `occurrences : tree → #{[root-address path\n918: node-address]}`, one row per place, is the structural half of the join\n919: between rows and the occurrence-keyed side tables of §2.5. It is a pure\n920: function of the tree, computable from either side of the round trip, so\n921: it carries no origin; a query fixes the origin from the composition's\n922: side (which batch, which event) and joins on `[root-address path]`\n923: within that origin. It is computed by the same walk that computes the\n924: projection.\n925: \n926: ### 6.2 Segment rows\n927: \n928: `project-segment : vector → [[pc tag & ops] …]`, the instruction vector with\n929: pc prepended. This relation is per segment: pcs collide across segments,\n930: and source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple slot\n931: (`datom.world.md:34`). A query over several segments takes them as\n932: separate sources (`:in $ $2 …`) or uses the **segment-qualified form**\n933: `[segment-address pc tag & ops]`, which adds one to every tag's arity\n934: uniformly and keeps per-tag arity fixed. Bare-pc rows from different\n935: segments are never unioned into one relation.\n936: \n937: ### 6.3 `q` over both, and the mechanics it rests on\n938: \n939: `q` accepts these relations through `query/relation`\n940: (`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`), whose contents are an arbitrary\n941: mixed-dimensional tuple collection. A pattern clause is exact-arity\n942: positional unification: `eval-pattern-clause` (`query.cljc:894-907`) parses\n943: the pattern (`query.cljc:419-436`), and on the general path unifies every\n944: row through `unify-slots` (`query.cljc:799-809`), which first requires\n945: `tuple-shape-matches?` (`query.cljc:439-441`) and then unifies slot by\n946: slot. A final `& ?tail` binds the remaining slots as a vector and `& _`\n947: ignores them (`query.cljc:431-436`, `:807-808`). The 3-slot EAV fast path\n948: (`query.cljc:899`) engages only when the source carries a fact index, which\n949: `fact-relation` (`query.cljc:172-177`) and the datom views supply; the code\n950: relations never do, so every code pattern takes the general path by\n951: construction.\n952: \n953: Verified shapes, each of which is a rule a query author may rely on:\n954: \n955: - **Per-tag selection.** `[?id :variable ?name]` matches only arity-3 rows.\n956: - **Joins across arities.** `[?site :application ?op _ _] [?op :variable\n957:   ?f]` joins an arity-5 row to an arity-3 row on the address.\n958: - **Operand membership** goes through a predicate: `[(member? ?operands\n959:   ?x)]` with `member?` supplied under `:fns`; predicate arguments must\n960:   already be bound.\n961: - **Ledger as-of** (§8.6) and **ref-to-code joins** (§6.4) run in one\n962:   query, the ref pattern on the fast path and the code pattern on the\n963:   general path.\n964: \n965: Two gotchas are rules:\n966: \n967: - `match` (`query.cljc:520-528`) is constant-or-wildcard selection:\n968:   `slots-match?` (`query.cljc:444-449`) treats only `_`, nil, and the\n969:   internal free marker as wildcards (`query.cljc:409-411`), so a `?var`\n970:   written in a `match` pattern is compared as a constant and binds nothing.\n971:   Use `q` to bind.\n972: - `:fns` keys are quoted symbols: `eval-fn-clause` looks the clause head up\n973:   as a symbol (`query.cljc:1099`), and the builtins merge under the same\n974:   keys (`query.cljc:1436-1438`). `{:fns {'member? f}}`, as\n975:   `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:228` writes it.\n976: \n977: ### 6.4 Refs joined by address\n978: \n979: A ref is a datom whose `v` is an address (§8). A name is an entity carrying\n980: `:yin/name`; joining refs to code is a join on the address value:\n981: \n982: ```clojure\n983: ;; every call site, in the tree currently named `my.ns/f`, whose operator is `+`\n984: [:find ?root ?path\n985:  :in $refs $code $occ\n986:  :where [$refs ?e :yin/name my.ns/f]          ; bind the name entity\n987:         [$refs ?e :yin/code ?root]            ; 3-slot, fast path, current view\n988:         [$code ?site :application ?op _ _]    ; general path\n989:         [$code ?op :variable +]\n990:         [$occ ?root ?path ?site]]             ; every place that content occurs in this tree\n991: ```\n992: \n993: `$refs` is a `current` view (§8.6), so the name resolves to its current\n994: address. The result names places, not contents: the occurrence relation\n995: turns one shared site row into one result per place.\n996: \n997: ### 6.5 What goes to the datom projection instead\n998: \n999: A tree also projects to datoms, `[e a v t m]` with tempids minted in\n1000: preorder and one extra fact `[e :yin/address addr]` per node so the\n1001: projection joins back to refs. This is the successor of `ast->datoms`\n1002: (`v2.cljc:372-482`) and is the *projection path* of §7.1. It exists for\n1003: three queries the flat rows deliberately do not serve:\n1004: \n1006: | Query | Why the datom projection |\n1008: | attribute-dimension queries with the attribute unbound, `[?e ?a ?v]` | a flat row has no attribute slot; \"every fact about this node\" is an EAV question |\n1010: | `pull` and `entity-attrs` navigation (`query.cljc:715`, `:1481`) | they navigate entity → attribute → value and reverse attrs |\n1012: | scale indexing under covered indexes (`dao.space.index`) | the covered-index publisher and `open-published!` are EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET over datoms |\n1013: | | (`query.cljc:452-476`) |\n1015: \n1016: The datom projection never becomes an identity: its `e` values are local,\n1017: and the only durable name in it is the `:yin/address` value.\n1018: \n1019: ---\n1020: \n1021: ## §7 The VM around it\n1022: \n1023: ### 7.1 Load paths\n1024: \n1025: Direct row loading is primary. As `dao.stream` observers, evaluators receive batches natively from the stream. A batch on the program stream is the\n1026: canonical row set of one tree (AST medium) or one canonical instruction\n1027: vector (code medium), and the evaluator's loader takes it as is:\n1028: \n1030: | Evaluator | Loader input | What loading does |\n1032: | walker | map AST | natively reads map AST from stream topic without conversion — set `:program` and `:control` to the map. |\n1033: | | | |\n1034: | | | |\n1036: | semantic | instruction vector | validate (§7.5), decode positional operands into the image (the `case` of `semantic.cljc:563-593` reading `(nth tuple i)`|\n1037: | | | instead of attribute maps), store under `:code` with the address as the alias column UCF §7.3.4 requires |\n1039: | semantic, | row set | `lower` (§5.2) then the row above; composed by the composition exactly as `linearize/ast-loader` is today |\n1040: | AST medium | | (`linearize.cljc:269-286`, chosen in `src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc:63-68`) |\n1042: \n1043: Datom-batch loading is the projection path: a batch of `:yin/*` datoms is\n1044: projected to rows by the datom→row half of the §9.1 codec pair and then\n1045: loaded as above; a batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms is decoded to a vector\n1046: by UCF §7.3.4's projection rule and then loaded as above. The projection\n1047: path exists for media that carry datoms and for the datom projection of\n1048: §6.5; it is not a second loader and it runs the same validator (§7.2).\n1049: \n1050: ### 7.2 Conformance: both paths, one validator, one image\n1051: \n1052: **This section supersedes the sentence in UCF §7.3.4 that names the\n1053: projection path as the reference path.** Under the owner's ruling the\n1054: direct path is primary and the reference; the projection path is derived\n1055: from it. Everything else in §7.3.4 stands.\n1056: \n1057: Both paths invoke the same validator (§7.4 for rows, §7.5 for vectors)\n1058: before anything is registered, and the conformance obligation has four\n1059: parts:\n1060: \n1061: 1. **The round-trip law.** For every corpus program, `map → rows → map`\n1062:    is the identity on the canonical map AST, and `rows → map → rows` is\n1063:    the identity on the rows. This is the both-paths obligation restated\n1064:    under the flat-row ontology: the projection is total and lossless,\n1065:    and each direction is tested against the other over the corpus. The\n1066:    corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, which is what makes\n1067:    this testable now.\n1068: 2. **Valid corpus, same result.** For every corpus program, loading the\n1069:    rows directly and loading their datom projection yield the same walker\n1070:    `:program` value; loading the instruction vector directly and loading\n1071:    its datom projection yield the same image. Keyword store keys\n1072:    (`parity_test.cljc:99`) and numeric store keys are part of the corpus\n1073:    and must round-trip on both paths, so the `key` kind is exercised as\n1074:    the `data` domain it is (§2.2), not as the two types the corpus\n1075:    happened to use.\n1076: 3. **Malformed input, same refusal.** For a published set of malformed\n1077:    vectors, each violating one rule of §7.5, and malformed row sets, each\n1078:    violating one rule of §7.4, both paths refuse with the same defect\n1079:    (rule and pc, or rule and path). A correctly hashed `[[:jump 9]]` is in\n1080:    the set. Image equality on valid input does not establish this; the\n1081:    malformed set does.\n1082: 4. **Several segments loaded.** Loading two or more segments through each\n1083:    path yields the same `:code` map and the same address alias column, and\n1084:    a second segment claiming a live local id fails identically on both.\n1085: \n1086: The obligation fails exactly when the dictionary and the datom schema\n1087: have drifted apart, which is the property that makes it the VM's own test\n1088: of this design.\n1089: \n1090: ### 7.3 Validation is the tuple grammar\n1091: \n1092: A tuple loader validates before it decodes, and validation is the §2 table\n1093: plus the structural rules a segment needs, nothing else. Both checkers\n1094: mirror `code/well-formed?` (`code.cljc:155-180`): each returns nil or the\n1095: first defect, rules run in order, and each rule may assume the earlier ones\n1096: held.\n1097: \n1098: ### 7.4 Row rules\n1099: \n1100: Defects name a path from the root row (§2.5), or the row's own id for a\n1101: row nothing reaches.\n1102: \n1104: | Rule | Defect when |\n1106: | `:tag` | the element after the id is not a tag of §2.3 |\n1108: | `:arity` | the count differs from the tag's body arity plus one |\n1110: | `:slot-kind` | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not an address, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of addresses, a `data` or |\n1111: | | `key` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`, a `sym` slot that is not a symbol, a `syms` slot that is not a vector |\n1112: | | of symbols |\n1114: | `:saturation` | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil |\n1116: | `:id-resolves` | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set |\n1118: | `:acyclic` | following child ids from a row revisits a row already on its own path |\n1120: | `:root-reachable` | a row of the loaded set is not reachable from the root row |\n1122: The three reference rules are the row counterpart of §7.5's\n1123: `:target-bounds`: a row's child slots are references, and they must\n1124: resolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n1125: must land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n1126: address check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n1127: ids, so a cycle would break the hash (§4.1) — and the rule stands\n1128: regardless, so that a load taking ids as given still names the defect.\n1129: \n1130: There is no encoder-domain rule: §4.2's residuals are disclosed value\n1131: shapes (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte arrays), not a\n1132: restricted admission domain, and ordinary identity uses are unblocked.\n1133: \n1134: ### 7.5 Vector rules\n1135: \n1136: Defects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,\n1137: dense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n1138: vector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the\n1139: UCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.\n1140: \n1142: | Rule | Defect when |\n1144: | `:nonempty` | the vector has no instructions |\n1146: | `:mnemonic` | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) |\n1148: | `:arity` | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2 |\n1150: | `:operand-kind` | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values or `:store-get`/`:store-put` keys fail `plain-data?` (the `key` kind of §2.2), a |\n1151: | | a `:var` name is not a symbol, a `:closure` params is not a vector of symbols, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym` |\n1152: | | prefix is not a string |\n1154: | `:saturation` | a saturated operand (`:gensym` prefix, `:stream-make` buffer, `:call` tail?, `:ffi-call` argc) is nil |\n1156: | `:target-bounds` | a `:jump`/`:branch-false` target or a `:closure` body is not an integer in `[0, length)` |\n1158: | `:terminator` | the last instruction is not in `code/terminators` (`code.cljc:19-21`) |\n1160: | `:argc` | a `:call`/`:ffi-call` argc is not a non-negative integer |\n1162: \n1163: There is no vector-level binding rule for `:var`: nesting is a property of the AST, checked by §7.4; an unbound `:var` name at run time evaluates to `nil` (§7.7.2).\n1164: \n1165: The address check (the vector hashes to the address it claims) is UCF\n1166: §7.3.4's and runs before these rules whenever an address is claimed; a\n1167: correct hash is never structural validation. The failure outcome of both\n1168: checkers on the UCF lowering path is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc or\n1169: path, as §7.3.4 already specifies; on a local load it is a load error\n1170: naming the same.\n1171: \n1172: ### 7.6 The hot loop never queries\n1173: \n1174: The walker dispatches on `(:type node)` and reads named fields of the map\n1175: AST it reconstructed at load; the semantic VM runs `(aget code pc)` over\n1176: an image decoded once at load (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.1). Neither touches\n1177: a relation, a ref, an address\n1178: index, or a projection while stepping. Projection and query are load-time\n1179: and tooling-time operations. This is the rule that keeps\n1180: `yin.vm.semantic.md` §6's cost model intact under this design: queryability\n1181: is paid once at load, or by whoever asks, never per step.\n1182: \n1183: ### 7.7 Dependency closure is Datalog over the tuples\n1184: \n1185: UCF §7.6.1 computes `:yin.k/requires` by a conservative fixed point over\n1186: values, code, names, and modules. The **code part** of that fixed point is\n1187: the queries below, run over the flat rows of every reachable tree\n1188: and the segment-qualified rows (§6.2) of every reachable segment. The\n1189: value, name-satisfaction, and module parts remain the walk §7.6.1\n1190: specifies; these queries supply their inputs and do not replace them.\n1191: \n1192: Extraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable\n1193: trees):\n1194: \n1195: ```clojure\n1196: ;; Free-name extraction is a query, not a tag match (§4.5): a :variable\n1197: ;; occurrence's name is a free-name obligation unless it is bound by an\n1198: ;; enclosing :lambda on ITS OWN occurrence path. This must be an\n1199: ;; occurrence-relation query (§2.5's [origin root-address path]), not a\n1200: ;; row-only one: content-addressed sharing means one :variable row can\n1201: ;; have several occurrences, and a row-only rule that walks row parent\n1202: ;; edges instead of per-occurrence paths can find a binder through one\n1203: ;; occurrence's edge and wrongly call every occurrence of that row bound\n1204: ;; — undercounting a real dependency, which UCF §7.6.1's conservative\n1205: ;; fixed point must never do (§4.5). See `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`'s\n1206: ;; occurrence-aware rule set for the verified version (scoped per root —\n1207: ;; a production query must additionally constrain every rule to one\n1208: ;; root-address, since an unscoped rule set can otherwise resolve a\n1209: ;; free name in one tree against a binder in a different reachable\n1210: ;; tree); the shape here is illustrative, not the exact syntax.\n1211: [:find ?name :in $ast $occ % ?root\n1212:  :where [$occ ?root ?path ?v] [?v :variable ?name] (not (bound? ?root ?path ?name))]\n1213: \n1214: ;; store keys read or written by code\n1215: [:find ?key :in $ast :where (or [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]\n1216:                                 [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _])]\n1217: \n1218: ;; FFI ops\n1219: [:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]\n1220: \n1221: ;; parked ids named by resume\n1222: [:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]\n1223: \n1224: ;; syntax that raises an effect: the tag, to be normalized by the footprint table below\n1225: [:find ?tag :in $ast\n1226:  :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]\n1227:         [(contains? #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next\n1228:                       :stream/close :dao.stream.apply/call} ?tag)]]\n1229: ```\n1230: \n1231: Extraction over segments (`$code` is the union of segment-qualified rows):\n1232: \n1233: ```clojure\n1234: ;; likewise over segment-qualified rows: derive free names from the\n1235: ;; instruction vector's own scoping, no separate opcode needed. Unlike\n1236: ;; AST rows, instructions are positionally addressed within one segment\n1237: ;; (segment-address, pc), not content-shared sub-vector by sub-vector, so\n1238: ;; the mixed-occurrence risk §4.5 documents for AST rows may not apply\n1239: ;; here the same way — this is unverified (§5's lowering, `linearize.cljc`\n1240: ;; `lower`, is not implemented yet, so no test exercises this query);\n1241: ;; confirm this reasoning when that work lands rather than assuming it.\n1242: [:find ?name :in $code % :where [$code _ _ :var ?name] (not (bound? ?name))]\n1243: [:find ?key  :in $code :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]\n1244:                                   [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])]\n1245: [:find ?op   :in $code :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]]\n1246: [:find ?pid  :in $code :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]]\n1247: [:find ?mn   :in $code\n1248:  :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]\n1249:         [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next\n1250:                       :stream-close :ffi-call} ?mn)]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"The root-scoping paragraph now clearly separates the valid single-root demonstration from production requirements. A few issues remain: the old “unbound variable evaluates to nil” sentence is unchanged, and the new sharing paragraph repeats “seq/list” and makes a broader encoder guarantee than §4.2 supports.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\").read_text().splitlines()\nfor i in range(1250,1670):\n x=s[i]\n if x.startswith(\\\"+\\\"):continue\n print(f\\\"{i+1}: \\\"+(\\\" \\\".join(x.split()) if x.startswith(\\\"|\\\") else x))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\").read_text().splitlines()\nfor i in range(1250,1670):\n x=s[i]\n if x.startswith(\\\"+\\\"):continue\n print(f\\\"{i+1}: \\\"+(\\\" \\\".join(x.split()) if x.startswith(\\\"|\\\") else x))'\"","aggregated_output":"1251: ```\n1252: \n1253: Store keys are extracted as values of the `key` kind (§2.2), whatever\n1254: their type, and are compared with the store slice's keys by value.\n1255: \n1256: #### 7.7.1 Effect normalization: the footprint table\n1257: \n1258: Syntax names effects by tag, segments by mnemonic, and profiles and\n1259: `:yin.k/requires` by **effect identifier** (`:stream/put`, UCF §7.6.1's\n1260: `:yin.k/effects`). The reference machine performs the mnemonic-to-effect\n1261: mapping in its hot loop (`semantic.cljc:360-403`: `:stream-make` raises\n1262: `{:effect :stream/make}`, `:stream-put` raises `{:effect :stream/put}`, and\n1263: so on; `:ffi-call` goes through the FFI pair, `semantic.cljc:405-420`).\n1264: That mapping is lifted out of the machine into a **footprint table**,\n1265: versioned with the execution contract stamp (UCF §7.3.3) because the\n1266: effect a mnemonic raises is part of that contract. Extraction applies the\n1267: table **before** any union with callable-profile effects, so the union is\n1268: over one vocabulary. The table for contract `\"v2\"`:\n1269: \n1271: | Syntax tag | Mnemonic | Effect identifiers | Other requirement contributed |\n1272: | | | contributed | |\n1274: | `:stream/make | `:stream-make` | `#{:stream/make}` | — |\n1275: | ` | | | |\n1277: | `:stream/put` | `:stream-put` | `#{:stream/put}` | — |\n1279: | `:stream/curs | `:stream-cursor` | `#{:stream/cursor} | — |\n1280: | or` | | ` | |\n1282: | `:stream/next | `:stream-next` | `#{:stream/next}` | — |\n1283: | ` | | | |\n1285: | `:stream/clos | `:stream-close` | `#{:stream/close}` | — |\n1286: | e` | | | |\n1288: | `:dao.stream. | `:ffi-call op` | `#{}`: an FFI call | `op` into `:yin.k/ffi-ops`, which is a **receiver capability requirement**: the resumer must hold its |\n1289: | apply/call | | is not an effect | own FFI pair (`ffi/require-call-pair!` is checked before every call, `semantic.cljc:405-415`) and a |\n1290: | op` | | kind | bridge handler for `op`. The pair's store keys (`v2.cljc:135-147`) are **never** a store-slice |\n1291: | | | | requirement: UCF §7.6.2 excludes the pair from the slice and the resumer installs its own; outstanding |\n1292: | | | | calls route per UCF §7.4.3 |\n1294: | `:vm/park` | `:park` | `#{}`: | — |\n1295: | | | scheduler-internal | |\n1297: | `:vm/resume | `:resume pid` | `#{}` | `pid` into the parked-record obligation |\n1298: | pid` | | | |\n1300: | `:vm/gensym` | `:gensym` | `#{}`: the id | — |\n1301: | | | counter is machine | |\n1302: | | | state | |\n1304: | `:vm/store-ge | `:store-get k`, | `#{}` | `k` into the store-slice requirement |\n1305: | t k`, | `:store-put k` | | |\n1306: | `:vm/store-pu | | | |\n1307: | t k` | | | |\n1309: | `:vm/current- | `:current-continuation | `#{}` | — |\n1310: | continuation` | ` | | |\n1312: | `:literal`, | `:const`, `:var`, | `#{}` from syntax; | — |\n1313: | `:variable`, | `:closure`, `:push`, | a call's effects | |\n1314: | `:lambda`, | `:call`, `:return`, | are its callee's | |\n1315: | `:application | `:jump`, | profile effects | |\n1316: | `, `:if` | `:branch-false`, | (§7.7.2) | |\n1317: | | `:halt` | | |\n1319: \n1320: Every tag and every mnemonic has a row, so \"no external effect\" is an\n1321: explicit `#{}`, never an absence. `:variable`/`:var`'s \"Other requirement\"\n1322: column is blank here because this table is unconditional per tag, and a\n1323: `:variable` row's contribution to the name obligation is conditional on\n1324: whether it is free (§4.5, §7.7.2) — that determination is a query over\n1325: `$ast`/`$code`, not a per-tag footprint fact, and is computed by the\n1326: extraction queries in §7.7 rather than this table. The conformance\n1327: obligation: for every corpus tree, the requirement set computed from the\n1328: tree and the one computed from its lowered segment are **equal** in every\n1329: field of `:yin.k/requires`. A tag or mnemonic outside the table is\n1330: `:yin.k/undecodable`, the same outcome the validators give it.\n1331: \n1332: #### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\n1333: \n1334: A name extracted from code is an **obligation** when it is a **free**\n1335: `:variable` row's `name` — free per the query of §4.5, not per a\n1336: separate tag. This design preserves named variables across the Semantic\n1337: Tuple boundary, and keeps a single resolution path: the evaluator looks up\n1338: every `:variable` name through `resolve-var`'s existing precedence, env →\n1339: store → primitives → modules (`engine.cljc:46-58`, `ast_walker.cljc:361-363`,\n1340: `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this is already what today's code does, and\n1341: this design does not change it. What changes is only how the obligation\n1342: set is computed: not by scanning for a distinct tag, but by the §4.5/§7.7\n1343: query over which `:variable` names are free.\n1344: \n1345: **Under-arity calls leave missing parameters bound to `nil`, not unbound.**\n1346: An under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an\n1347: over-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. This\n1348: is a deliberate execution-contract change from today's `zipmap`-based\n1349: binding (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`;\n1350: `semantic.cljc:200`), which currently leaves a missing parameter name\n1351: absent from the extended environment rather than explicitly `nil` — so it\n1352: can currently fall through to whatever the closure's own captured\n1353: environment (or, transitively, the store/primitives/modules chain) has\n1354: under that name. After the fix, a missing parameter is `nil` and shadows\n1355: any such fallthrough within the closure's body. This is independent of\n1356: §4.5's free/bound decision: it is about parameter binding at closure\n1357: application, not about resolving a name to a value.\n1358: \n1359: Every retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by\n1360: profile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its\n1361: profile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`\n1362: contributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`\n1363: because their profiles say so. Where a retained name has no profile at the\n1364: emitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every\n1365: possible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never\n1366: makes a `:complete` result wrong.\n1367: \n1368: This rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its\n1369: sentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n1370: value carries\" is the unsound test, and this document states the\n1371: correct rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same\n1372: (§10.8).\n1373: \n1374: #### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses\n1375: \n1376: The unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an\n1377: address. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured\n1378: environments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n1379: own environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n1380: contribute different work items; the name obligations of the segment are the same for both. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the\n1381: closure's captured environment, and nothing else. This context\n1382: contributes values (such as closures or streams bound within it), not\n1383: name discharge. Because values are finite, the set of contexts is finite\n1384: and the iteration terminates.\n1385: \n1386: - **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected\n1387:   once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is\n1388:   analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.\n1389: - **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the\n1390:   carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3); a missing one is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`\n1391:   naming the id.\n1392: - **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the\n1393:   K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait\n1394:   contribute work items and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies; a\n1395:   closure contributes `[its segment address, its captured-env context]`.\n1396: - **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets\n1397:   `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.\n1398: \n1399: **Convergence** is reached when one full pass adds nothing to any of:\n1400: the work-item set, the obligation set, the discovered\n1401: value set (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), the store-key\n1402: requirement, the FFI-op requirement, the effect set after normalization\n1403: (§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new\n1404: context, a new profile fact, or a new footprint continues the iteration\n1405: even when no new address appeared.\n1406: \n1407: Discovery is `:complete` only when every reachable work item was\n1408: analyzed, every free-`:variable` obligation was discharged by the store slice by key or retained\n1409: as a profiled primitive or module requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module\n1410: footprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\n1411: fetched and `:incomplete` otherwise. Recasting the walk as Datalog changes\n1412: where the facts come from, not the conservatism of the closure.\n1413: \n1414: UCF §7.6.1 has the same defect in its own terms: \"repeats until nothing\n1415: new is reachable\" is stated over segments and values, and its walk of \"a\n1416: newly fetched segment\" is per address. It needs this same amendment\n1417: (§10.8).\n1418: \n1419: Because the queries are over data, they answer for a tree that has never\n1420: run, which is the property §7.6.1 names.\n1421: \n1422: ---\n1423: \n1424: ## §8 The ledger and refs\n1425: \n1426: ### 8.1 Two notations, one relation\n1427: \n1428: The ledger is one logical relation with rows `[t op address ref]`, ruling\n1429: 6's shape: `t` is the ledger's clock, `op` is drawn from an open\n1430: vocabulary, `address` is a content address, and `ref` is the row's other\n1431: argument, a name entity or a second address. This document writes ledger\n1432: facts in that notation when it speaks of the relation.\n1433: \n1434: The **persistent representation** of a row is datoms, and a datom is bound\n1435: by `datom.md`'s contract: `e`, `t`, and `m` are stream-local, `e` is a\n1436: non-negative integer, `m` is an integer, and `v` is the only slot that may\n1437: hold a foreign reference (`datom.md:140-180`). The transactor enforces\n1438: this: `local-datom?` (`src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:42-58`) rejects a\n1439: non-integer `e` or `m`, and `pad-datom`\n1440: (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:74-90`) rejects a caller-supplied `t`\n1441: and stamps its own. Therefore a content address never appears in `e`, an\n1442: op keyword never appears in `m`, and `t` is never chosen by the writer.\n1443: The mapping between notations is:\n1444: \n1446: | Logical slot | Persistent carrier |\n1448: | `t` | the datom `t` the transactor stamped |\n1450: | `op` | for naming rows: the reserved validity marker in `m` (`:db/assert` 1, `:db/retract` 0); for every other op: the `:yin.ledger/op` value of a **ledger |\n1451: | | event entity** (§8.2) |\n1453: | `address` | a `v` slot |\n1455: | `ref` | for naming rows: the local name entity in `e`; for address-to-address rows: a second `v` slot of the same event entity |\n1457: \n1458: Anything written in the `[t op address ref]` notation below is a logical\n1459: row; anything written as `[e a v t m]` is transactor input, with `e` a\n1460: local integer or tempid, `t` nil at write time, and `m` a reserved marker.\n1461: \n1462: ### 8.2 Ledger records and ledger events\n1463: \n1464: Every non-naming ledger fact is first a **ledger record**: a plain map,\n1465: content-addressed by `segment-key`, stored in `dao.jing` like any other content.\n1466: Record shapes:\n1467: \n1469: | Op | Record | Meaning |\n1471: | `:derive` | `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree-addr | `output` was computed from `input` by `function` under the lowering profile (§5.2.1) |\n1472: | | :yin.ledger/output seg-addr :yin.ledger/function | |\n1473: | | fn-name :yin.ledger/profile profile-map}` | |\n1475: | `:expand` | `{:yin.ledger/op :expand :yin.ledger/input tree-in | one expansion attempt; exactly one of `:yin.ledger/origin` (initial) and `:yin.ledger/parent` |\n1476: | | :yin.ledger/call-root tree-in :yin.ledger/call-path | (nested) is present; failures carry `:yin.ledger/error` instead of `:yin.ledger/output` (§8.4). |\n1477: | | path :yin.ledger/origin [:source medium batch j]` | `:yin.ledger/attempt` is the portable attempt identity of §8.4.1, scoped to the expander |\n1478: | | **or** `:yin.ledger/parent parent-record-addr` | incarnation, so two expanders or two executions of one expander observing one call never mint one |\n1479: | | `:yin.ledger/output tree-out :yin.ledger/macro | record. The record holds no log-local id: a nested attempt names its parent by the parent's |\n1480: | | lambda-addr :yin.ledger/attempt [incarnation | record **address**, so the record is minted only after the parent's record exists |\n1481: | | counter]}` | |\n1483: | `:publish` | `{:yin.ledger/op :publish :yin.ledger/subject | a checkpoint occurrence was published as the UCF value at `output` (UCF §7.3.4) |\n1484: | | occurrence-id :yin.ledger/output ucf-addr}` | |\n1486: \n1487: A record is a content-addressed value, so it is immutable, addressable, and verifiable,\n1488: and it is what \"the hash chain\" is made of: a record names its input and\n1489: output by address, and the record's own address is what the ledger\n1490: publishes. Walking the chain is following record addresses; code content\n1491: never has to know its predecessor.\n1492: \n1493: A record enters the ledger as a **ledger event entity**, a local entity\n1494: (id ≥ `dao.datom/first-user-id`, `datom.cljc:27-40`) with the record's\n1495: fields as attributes and the record's address as one more:\n1496: \n1497: ```clojure\n1498: [[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op       :derive]\n1499:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input    tree-addr]\n1500:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output   seg-addr]\n1501:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower]\n1502:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-v1\" :yin.code/contract \"v2\" :yin.k/version 0}]\n1503:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record   record-addr]]     ; ev is a tempid; m defaults to :db/assert\n1504: ```\n1505: \n1506: The profile is carried as the structured map, exactly as it appears in the\n1507: record. `local-datom?` constrains `e`, `a`, `t`, and `m` and places no\n1508: restriction on `v` (`datom.cljc:42-58`), and today's schema already\n1509: carries a vector in `v`; no string\n1510: rendering of the profile is defined.\n1511: \n1512: Event operation and fact validity are separate by construction. The op is\n1513: a namespaced keyword in a `v` slot, globally meaningful without a\n1514: reserved id, which is `datom.md`'s own rule for built-ins that live in `a`\n1515: and `v` (`datom.md:214-217`); the datoms of the event carry the ordinary\n1516: `:db/assert` marker and fold as ordinary facts. No reserved `m` id is\n1517: allocated, because reserved ids have one global meaning everywhere\n1518: (`datom.md:200-212`) and a composition cannot mint one. Where a\n1519: composition wants an event to be the provenance of a naming fact, the\n1520: naming datom's `m` names the event entity through the established\n1521: metadata-entity mechanism, an id ≥ 16 carrying `:db/op` and provenance\n1522: (`datom.cljc:2-7`, `datom.md:172-180`; the transactor's map-`m` input at\n1523: `src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:15-29`). A fact whose `m` names a\n1524: metadata entity is live under the current fold, which removes only\n1525: `:db/retract` markers (`query.cljc:76-103`); a validity op other than\n1526: assert or retract is expressed by the reserved marker, never by the\n1527: event, so the fold needs no knowledge of the open vocabulary. Interpreting\n1528: a metadata entity's `:db/op` for Datomic-visible validity is the\n1529: prerequisite `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names and is outside this\n1530: design.\n1531: \n1532: The vocabulary is open: a composition that needs a new op declares a new\n1533: keyword and a record shape. A reader dispatches on the ops it understands\n1534: (`[?ev :yin.ledger/op :derive]`) and does not see the others, which is a\n1535: property of the query it writes, not of the fold.\n1536: \n1537: ### 8.3 Rows of the one relation\n1538: \n1540: | Logical row | Persistent form |\n1542: | `[t :assert tree-addr name]` — a name refers | `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]` |\n1543: | to a tree | |\n1545: | `[t :retract tree-addr name]` — the name no | `[:db/retract name :yin/code tree-addr]`, followed by the new assertion |\n1546: | longer does | |\n1548: | `[t :derive seg-addr tree-addr]` — a segment | a `:derive` event entity (§8.2); direction: `input` is the tree, `output` is the segment |\n1549: | was lowered from a tree | |\n1551: | `[t :assert seg-addr k-occurrence]` — a | `[:db/add occ :yin.k/segment seg-addr]` on the local occurrence entity |\n1552: | segment is the address a continuation names | |\n1554: | `[t :expand tree-out tree-in]` — a tree is | an `:expand` event entity (§8.4) |\n1555: | the expansion of another | |\n1557: | `[t :assert lambda-addr name]` under | not a ledger row at all: macro-ness is an occurrence-bound declaration beside the tree (§8.5); the ledger records only |\n1558: | `:yin.macro/definition` | the expander's harvest result, `[:db/add name :yin.macro/definition lambda-addr]`, for queries |\n1560: | `[t :publish ucf-addr occurrence]` — a | a `:publish` event entity; custody stays `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged (UCF §7.7.2), keyed by the same local |\n1561: | checkpoint occurrence was published | occurrence entity |\n1563: | `[t :assert tree-addr manifest]` — a module | `[:db/add manifest :yin.module/exports tree-addr]` |\n1564: | manifest pins a tree | |\n1566: \n1567: `:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name as a query alias for\n1568: \"the `:yin.ledger/input` of a `:derive` event whose output is this\n1569: address\" and is no longer an attribute of the segment (`v2.cljc:327`,\n1570: `linearize.cljc:212`).\n1571: \n1572: ### 8.4 Expansion events keep their identity\n1573: \n1574: `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 records one event per attempt, successful or not,\n1575: including repeated identical expansions, and links the event to its output\n1576: (`yin.vm.macro.md:666-709`). This design keeps that contract: the\n1577: `:expand` event entity **is** that event, one per attempt, and the address\n1578: rows are attributes of it, not a replacement for it:\n1579: \n1580: ```clojure\n1581: [[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op         :expand]\n1582:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input      tree-in-addr]        ; the tree containing the call\n1583:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-root  tree-in-addr]        ; the call occurrence, as values:\n1584:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-path  path]                ;   root address and structural path\n1585:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/origin     [:source medium batch j]]   ; INITIAL expansion only: a value\n1586:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent-event parent-ev]         ; NESTED expansion only: a declared ref, log-local\n1587:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent     parent-record-addr]  ; NESTED expansion only: the parent's record address, portable\n1588:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output     tree-out-addr]       ; on success\n1589:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/macro      lambda-addr]         ; the macro's content\n1590:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/attempt    [incarnation n]]     ; portable attempt identity, §8.4.1\n1591:  [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record     record-addr]         ; the addressed record of §8.2\n1592:  [:db/add ev :yin/source-batch      batch-id]            ; unchanged from §4.1\n1593:  [:db/add ev :yin/error             {...}]               ; on failure, unchanged\n1594:  [:db/add ev :yin/timestamp         ctx-t]]              ; unchanged\n1595: ```\n1596: \n1597: The split between local references and portable values follows\n1598: `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 exactly (`yin.vm.macro.md:692-708`), and it is\n1599: enforced by how the transactor resolves ids: `apply-tempid-map` replaces a\n1600: tempid in `v` **only when the whole value is a tempid on a declared\n1601: reference attribute** (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:168-185`); a\n1602: tempid nested inside a vector is left as it is. Therefore:\n1603: \n1604: - an **initial** expansion's call sits in admitted syntax, so it carries\n1605:   `:yin.ledger/origin`, a value `[:source medium batch j]` including the\n1606:   batch member index: the successor of `:yin/source-call` qualified by\n1607:   `:yin/source-batch`, which no transactor resolves, as today;\n1608: - a **nested** expansion's call sits in the output of the enclosing\n1609:   expansion, so it carries **two** links to the parent: `:yin.ledger/\n1610:   parent-event`, a declared reference attribute whose whole value is the\n1611:   parent event's id, the successor of `:yin/source-node` and resolved by\n1612:   the transactor within the log medium exactly as `:yin/source-node` is\n1613:   today; and `:yin.ledger/parent`, the parent's **record address**, which\n1614:   is a value and travels. The parent is complete before the child begins,\n1615:   because a nested expansion's input is the enclosing expansion's output\n1616:   (`yin.vm.macro.md:698-701`), so the parent's record address is known\n1617:   when the child's record is minted; no staging after allocation is\n1618:   needed, and the record (§8.2) never contains a log-local id, so it is\n1619:   hashed once, when built.\n1620: \n1621: A successful event carries exactly one of `:yin.ledger/origin` and the\n1622: parent pair; an admission failure carries neither. The chain `source →\n1623: event → output → event → output` of §4.1 is walked locally through\n1624: `:yin.ledger/parent-event` and portably through `:yin.ledger/parent`, and\n1625: two attempts that produced identical output trees are two events with two\n1626: ids and two record addresses, because their `:yin.ledger/attempt` values\n1627: differ by construction (§8.4.1), so a child names its parent without\n1628: ambiguity in either walk. `:yin/expansion-root` and `:yin/macro` of §4.1 are carried by\n1629: `:yin.ledger/output` and `:yin.ledger/macro` as addresses; the other event\n1630: attributes are unchanged. Because the *content* values are addresses, the\n1631: cross-media identity problem of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 does not arise for\n1632: content: the same tree has the same address on `program-in`,\n1633: `program-out`, and the log. The local event id remains log-local, as\n1634: `:yin/source-node` is today; the record address is what leaves the log.\n1635: \n1636: #### 8.4.1 Attempt identity is scoped to an expander incarnation\n1637: \n1638: A record's address is the hash of its value, so two attempts whose records\n1639: are value-equal are one record, whatever the encoder. A bare per-expander\n1640: counter does not prevent that: two expanders observing one medium, batch,\n1641: member, and call path, each at counter `0`, with one macro and one output,\n1642: would write one record; so would one expander restarted and replaying the\n1643: batch. The governing contract records every attempt separately\n1644: (`yin.vm.macro.md:668-669`), so the attempt identity must be unique per\n1645: attempt across expanders and across executions. It is:\n1646: \n1647: ```clojure\n1648: :yin.ledger/attempt [incarnation counter]\n1649: ```\n1650: \n1651: - **The incarnation** is a value held in the expander's process state\n1652:   `ctx` (`yin.vm.macro.md:216-223`), as a new field `:incarnation`,\n1653:   threaded through every call exactly as `:alloc` and `:t` are. It is\n1654:   **allocated**, never observed: a cursor read reserves nothing, since a\n1655:   cursor is a position minted by the stream for observation\n1656:   (`dao.stream.md:471-477`) and the memory log's `:dao.stream/newest`\n1657:   cursor is simply its current value count\n1658:   (`src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:105-110`), so two constructions\n1659:   with no append between them read one position. Nor does committing a\n1660:   record on the log allocate anything: `prepare-tx` is a pure function of\n1661:   the history it is handed (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:189-207`),\n1662:   so two preparations against one history assign one permanent id, and\n1663:   `transact!` allocates only `t` under its lock and appends datoms that\n1664:   are already resolved (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:231-254`);\n1665:   two transactor values over one stream are a documented single-writer\n1666:   hazard, not a coordination (`dao.space.transactor.md`, T6). **The log\n1667:   therefore plays no part in incarnation identity.** The incarnation is a\n1668:   **composition-minted token**, the one mechanism for every construction:\n1669: \n1670:   ```clojure\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\").read_text().splitlines()\nfor i in range(1670,len(s)):\n x=s[i]\n if x.startswith(\\\"+\\\"):continue\n print(f\\\"{i+1}: \\\"+(\\\" \\\".join(x.split()) if x.startswith(\\\"|\\\") else x))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from pathlib import Path\ns=Path(\\\"docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md\\\").read_text().splitlines()\nfor i in range(1670,len(s)):\n x=s[i]\n if x.startswith(\\\"+\\\"):continue\n print(f\\\"{i+1}: \\\"+(\\\" \\\".join(x.split()) if x.startswith(\\\"|\\\") else x))'\"","aggregated_output":"1671:   :incarnation {:yin.expander/token <token>}\n1672:   ```\n1673: \n1674:   where `<token>` is a fresh random UUID minted by the host's library at\n1675:   the moment of construction (`random-uuid`, as the ring-buffer transport\n1676:   already mints its stream identity, `src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:27`).\n1677:   The uniqueness warranty is the composition's, discharged by the token's\n1678:   122 random bits: it covers two expanders on one log, two expanders on\n1679:   two logs, and one expander constructed twice, with or without a\n1680:   restart, and it needs no state that survives a restart, which is why a\n1681:   random token and not a counter is the rule; a durable counter would\n1682:   need an allocate-and-persist boundary of its own, which is the problem\n1683:   being avoided. No id allocated by any transactor, and no position of\n1684:   any stream, enters the incarnation.\n1685: - **Allocation retry.** Construction is all-or-nothing, as it is for a VM\n1686:   (`v2.cljc:21-23`): minting the token is the first act of construction,\n1687:   and a construction that fails afterward, for any reason, leaves no\n1688:   expander. A construction that is retried mints a **new token** and\n1689:   obtains a new incarnation; it never reuses or reconstructs an earlier\n1690:   one, because an incarnation is reachable only through the `ctx` that\n1691:   holds it, and a `ctx` that was not retained is gone. A token under\n1692:   which no attempt was ever recorded is harmless.\n1693: - **The counter** is a per-incarnation attempt counter in `ctx`, advanced\n1694:   once per attempt, initial or nested, successful or failed, exactly as\n1695:   `:alloc` advances (`yin.vm.macro.md:360-365`); admission-failure events\n1696:   advance it too, since they are attempts under §4.1.\n1697: - **A staged retry reuses the identity; a distinct execution receives\n1698:   another.** The expander stages `expand-batch`'s output and log payloads\n1699:   and retries a failed flush from the staged data without re-running the\n1700:   expansion (`yin.vm.macro.md:364-365`, `:765-773`): the record, and the\n1701:   attempt identity inside it, are part of what was staged, so the retry\n1702:   writes the same record to the same address, which is the idempotent\n1703:   re-publication the ledger wants. A restart constructs a new expander,\n1704:   which mints a new incarnation, so replaying a batch after a restart\n1705:   mints new attempt identities and new records: that is a distinct\n1706:   execution and is recorded as one. What separates the two cases is\n1707:   whether `ctx` survived: a retry runs under the `ctx` that produced the\n1708:   staged payload; a restart does not have it, and allocates (above).\n1709: \n1710: The parent-record chain of §8.4 is unchanged; it gains uniqueness from the\n1711: attempt identity inside every record.\n1712: \n1713: ### 8.5 Macro declarations travel beside the tree\n1714: \n1715: The `:macro?` flag is outside the canonical tree (§2.5). What replaces it\n1716: is a **declaration fact bound to an occurrence**, produced by the frontend\n1717: and consumed by the expander at admission. The governing contract admits\n1718: and harvests **every entity in the batch, in batch order, including\n1719: definitions disconnected from the execution root**\n1720: (`yin.vm.macro.md:229-249`). A single rooted tree cannot carry a\n1721: disconnected definition, so the composition between frontend and expander\n1722: is an **ordered batch**, not a tree:\n1723: \n1724: ```clojure\n1725: {:yin/batch         [<rows-0> <rows-1> … <rows-n>]   ; canonical row sets, one per admitted root or disconnected subgraph\n1726:  :yin/root          i                                ; index of the tree the evaluator runs\n1727:  :yin/declarations  #{[j path :yin.macro/definition] …}   ; the definition at path in tree j is a macro\n1728:  :yin/harvest       [#{[j path] …} #{[j' path'] …} …]}   ; one entry per ORIGINAL definition, in admission order;\n1729:                                                      ;   each entry is the set of [j path] occurrences of that definition\n1730: ```\n1731: \n1732: Rules:\n1733: \n1734: - **Harvest order is an explicit catalogue, not a traversal.** The\n1735:   governing contract harvests every definition in the batch **in the\n1736:   order its entities appeared in the admitted datom batch**\n1737:   (`yin.vm.macro.md:242-249`), and it admits any acyclic, internally\n1738:   resolved batch; nothing requires entity order to equal structural\n1739:   preorder, and a root may contain definitions A then B whose entities\n1740:   arrived as B then A. Vector order plus preorder therefore does **not**\n1741:   preserve that contract, and this document does not claim it does. The\n1742:   catalogue `:yin/harvest` is the preserved order: a vector with **one\n1743:   entry per original definition**, in the original admission order, each\n1744:   entry the set of `[j path]` occurrences at which that definition\n1745:   appears in the batch's trees, connected or not. Initial harvest\n1746:   (`yin.vm.macro.md` §3.1 step 2) iterates the catalogue, last wins; it\n1747:   never iterates the trees. For a batch converted from datoms by the §9.1\n1748:   adapter the catalogue is derived from entity order, so the legacy\n1749:   contract is preserved exactly. For a batch a tuple-native\n1750:   frontend emits, the frontend chooses the order it writes; the reference\n1751:   frontends write source order, which is the **declaration order** of\n1752:   `yin.vm.macro.md` §3.2 (`yin.vm.macro.md:298-304`: operands before the\n1753:   body of an immediately applied lambda). That is a new contract for a\n1754:   new producer, not a narrowing of the legacy one, and the two are kept\n1755:   distinct: post-expansion declaration order is what the expander uses\n1756:   for definitions its own output forms (§3.1 step 5), and it stays\n1757:   separate from the admission catalogue.\n1758: - **One original definition, many occurrences, one ordinal.** A legacy\n1759:   batch may hold one definition entity reached through several\n1760:   references: the codec emits a pre-assigned entity once and lets every\n1761:   referring site name it (`v2.cljc:394-411`, `seen-eids`), and\n1762:   `yang.clojure` produces exactly this for a defmacro lambda used at its\n1763:   definition and at call sites (`clojure.cljc:418-437`). Inlining that\n1764:   graph into the batch's trees produces several `[j path]` occurrences of\n1765:   one definition. The old harvest and declaration catalogue number the\n1766:   **entity**, giving it one declaration ordinal `k`, and a transformer\n1767:   may fabricate a stand-in that selects a catalogue entry by `k`\n1768:   (`yin.vm.macro.md:254-275`), so the ordinal is observable and\n1769:   duplicating it would be wrong. Therefore a catalogue entry is an\n1770:   **occurrence group**: the definition is harvested once, and the\n1771:   catalogue, not the trees, is the identity of the definition. The\n1772:   adapter derives groups from entity identity; a tuple-native frontend\n1773:   emits singleton groups, one per definition it writes, and if it writes\n1774:   one definition at several places it emits one group naming them all.\n1775: - **Two sequences: harvest order and declaration ordinals.** The\n1776:   governing contract numbers **only source macro definitions** into\n1777:   `:declared`, in harvest order, and replaces only those by stand-ins;\n1778:   plain definitions stay executable `yin/def` nodes and are forwarded\n1779:   untouched (`yin.vm.macro.md:254-278`). The harvest catalogue above\n1780:   contains every definition group, plain and macro alike, because harvest\n1781:   and redefinition need all of them in admission order. The **declaration\n1782:   catalogue** is a second, derived sequence: the macro-declared groups\n1783:   only (those whose occurrences carry `:yin.macro/definition`), in the\n1784:   relative order they have in the harvest catalogue. A macro group's\n1785:   declaration ordinal `k` is its index in the declaration catalogue, never\n1786:   its index in the harvest catalogue; every occurrence of that group is\n1787:   replaced by a stand-in carrying that same `:decl k` (§3.1 step 3), so a\n1788:   fabricated stand-in selects the same entry it would select under the\n1789:   legacy expander. A plain definition preceding macro `m` therefore leaves\n1790:   `m` at ordinal `0`, as today. Plain groups participate in harvest and\n1791:   redefinition (§3.1 step 2's last-wins over the harvest catalogue,\n1792:   including a plain redefinition removing a macro) and are **never**\n1793:   replaced by stand-ins; their occurrences remain ordinary `yin/def`\n1794:   syntax in the trees the evaluator receives.\n1795: - **The catalogue is validated at admission.** Every occurrence in every\n1796:   group must name a `(yin/def <literal sym> _)` node; every such node in\n1797:   the batch must appear in exactly one group; groups are non-empty and\n1798:   pairwise disjoint; every occurrence in a group must have the same\n1799:   `:literal` symbol and the same value-operand content address, since one\n1800:   original definition cannot have two bodies; every `:yin/declarations`\n1801:   entry must be a member of some group, and a group is declared as a whole\n1802:   (all of its occurrences carry the declaration or none do, which is\n1803:   automatic for a legacy entity with one flag). The declaration catalogue\n1804:   is derived from the validated harvest catalogue and the declarations,\n1805:   never carried separately, so it cannot disagree with either. A\n1806:   violation is an admission failure `{:kind :malformed-input :reason\n1807:   :harvest-catalogue}` naming the entry.\n1808: - **Binding.** A declaration `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` names the\n1809:   `:application` at `path` in tree `j`, and through the catalogue the\n1810:   original definition whose group contains `[j path]`; that node must be\n1811:   `(yin/def <literal sym> <lambda>)`, i.e., its reconstructed semantic map must have an `:operator` that is a `:variable` node naming `yin/def` (free at that site, per §4.5), its first operand a `:literal` symbol node, and its second operand a `:lambda` node. A\n1812:   declaration whose coordinates do not resolve to such a node is an\n1813:   admission failure, `{:kind :malformed-input :reason\n1814:   :stray-macro-declaration}`, the successor of §3.1 step 1's\n1815:   `:stray-macro-lambda`.\n1816: - **Marks are validated before they are stripped.** A frontend or the\n1817:   §9.1 adapter that converts a map AST carrying `:macro? true` must reject\n1818:   a flagged lambda anywhere but the value operand of a `yin/def` as\n1819:   `:stray-macro-lambda` **at conversion**, before the flag is removed;\n1820:   it may never strip such a flag and emit a plain lambda. A flag in the\n1821:   admitted position becomes the declaration at that tree and path. The\n1822:   quiet case `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 5 exists to prevent is therefore\n1823:   prevented at the same point it is today.\n1824: - **Ordering of harvest.** The catalogue's order; last wins, as §3.1\n1825:   step 2 rules. Vector order and preorder play no part in harvest.\n1826: - **Missing fact.** A `(yin/def sym v)` with no declaration at its\n1827:   coordinates is a plain definition. If `sym` is currently a macro, the\n1828:   plain definition removes it, exactly as §3.1 step 2 rules.\n1829: - **Scope.** Harvest is syntactic and whole-batch, as today; a declaration\n1830:   in an unexecuted branch or in a tree the root never reaches is in force\n1831:   for the batch. Declarations are per batch and never persist beyond the\n1832:   harvest that consumed them; the harvested store is the expander's state.\n1833: - **Redefinition.** Two batches that carry identical trees differ in\n1834:   behaviour only through their declarations, which is the intended\n1835:   separation: content is what the program is; being a macro is what this\n1836:   occurrence of a definition is *for*.\n1837: - **Evaluators see the root only.** The composition hands an evaluator\n1838:   `(nth batch root)` after expansion; a tree outside the root is expander\n1839:   input, never program.\n1840: \n1841: This replacement protocol, not the expander itself, is the blocker\n1842: (§10.5): tuple evaluation without macros needs none of it, and the v2\n1843: expander does not exist yet.\n1844: \n1845: ### 8.6 As-of queries are ledger views\n1846: \n1847: Current value of a name uses the implemented current-state interpreter,\n1848: which resolves the greatest `t` per `[e a v]` and then removes retractions\n1849: (`query/current`, `query.cljc:204-212`; the fold `current-state-seq`,\n1850: `query.cljc:76-103`):\n1851: \n1852: ```clojure\n1853: (q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]\n1854:    (query/current refs) 'my.ns/f)\n1855: ```\n1856: \n1857: Value of a name as of `?t` is the same view with its as-of argument, which\n1858: bounds visible datoms to `t ≤ ?t` before folding\n1859: (`query/current` two-arity, `query.cljc:210-212`; `bound-datoms`,\n1860: `query.cljc:68-73`):\n1861: \n1862: ```clojure\n1863: (q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]\n1864:    (query/current refs t) 'my.ns/f)\n1865: ```\n1866: \n1867: A hand-written negation over raw d5 rows is not the contract and is not\n1868: used: \"any retraction before `?t`\" wrongly removes an address that was\n1869: retracted and later reasserted, whereas the fold's latest-event-wins per\n1870: `[e a v]` keeps it. A query that must inspect raw rows selects the latest\n1871: event per `[e a v]` with `max ?t` first and interprets that row's `m`\n1872: second.\n1873: \n1874: Provenance walks are joins over event entities:\n1875: \n1876: ```clojure\n1877: ;; what was this segment lowered from, and what did that expand from\n1878: [:find ?src\n1879:  :in $ ?seg\n1880:  :where [?d :yin.ledger/op :derive] [?d :yin.ledger/output ?seg] [?d :yin.ledger/input ?tree]\n1881:         [?x :yin.ledger/op :expand] [?x :yin.ledger/output ?tree] [?x :yin.ledger/input ?src]]\n1882: ```\n1883: \n1884: ---\n1885: \n1886: ## §9 Migration surface\n1887: \n1888: ### 9.1 The boundary, not a sweep\n1889: \n1890: The walker stays on map ASTs exactly as built, retaining named variable evaluation. What changes is the\n1891: boundary around them — the projection in, the loader and the readers of\n1892: rows behind it. The semantics do not change; the boundary does.\n1893: \n1895: | Site | Change |\n1897: | walker, `ast_walker.cljc` | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. `:variable` arm (`:361-363`) is unchanged — still resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (§4.5). Closure application (`:191`, hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`) changes from `(zipmap params args)` to nil-filling missing params. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2). |\n1899: | loader, new | validate rows (§7.4), reconstruct the map AST: `rows → map` (§6.1), the successor of `datoms->ast` |\n1900: | | (`v2.cljc:494-559`) with content addresses in place of allocated ids |\n1902: | linearizer, | reads the row relation through the same `get-attr`-over-an-index shape it has today (`lower-node`'s `(get-attr e |\n1903: | `linearize.cljc:87-148`, | :yin/type)` becomes a lookup by row id); `ast-children` becomes a table lookup of `node`/`nodes` slot positions; |\n1904: | `:230-242` | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name` and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away |\n1906: | codec, `v2.cljc:372-559` | becomes `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`, the projection pair of §6.5 mapping Universal AST to Canonical Rows/Datoms. |\n1907: | | `:yin/root` and `:eid` removed; `:yin/address` added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n1909: | `code/mnemonics`, `code/well-formed?`, | `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) is unchanged (§4.5). `well-formed?` keeps judging datom batches on the projection path before projection; the shared vector validator of §7.5 runs on |\n1910: | `code.cljc:12-180` | both paths after it |\n1912: | `semantic/load-image`, | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; frame binding for `:closure` matches arguments to `params` by position, binding each to its name (§7.7.2); apply (`semantic.cljc:200`) changes from `zipmap` to nil-filling missing params; `:var` opcode is unchanged, resolving env → store → primitives → modules (§4.5); decodes the new operand (`:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator |\n1913: | `semantic.cljc:524-596` | |\n1915: | frontends | keep emitting map ASTs to the stream (as named universal ASTs). They do not perform the tuple projection. |\n1916: | `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`, | All projection to Semantic Tuples and side-tables is deferred to the Encoder Observer (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`). |\n1917: | `python.cljc`, `php.cljc` | |\n1918: | | never survive the boundary. The r7 adapter's ordering rules become the standing contract: first reject any `:macro? |\n1919: | | true` lambda that is not the value operand of a `yin/def` as `:stray-macro-lambda`, then turn each admitted one into|\n1920: | | a declaration at its tree index and path, then strip the flag |\n1922: | datom-batch adapter, | for media that carry datoms: derives `:yin/harvest` from entity order and entity identity while inlining, recording |\n1923: | standing | every `[j path]` at which each definition entity is emitted (the `datoms->ast` recursion, `v2.cljc:494-559`, |\n1924: | | extended to carry the path and the entity id, since today it tracks neither); one entity with several paths becomes |\n1925: | | one occurrence group; the adapter assigns no `:decl` values (§8.5) |\n1927: \n1928: The v1 lineage (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ast_walker_test.cljc`,\n1929: `test/yin/vm/ast_conversion_test.cljc`, `runtime_regression_test.cljc`) is\n1930: outside this design; it stays on its own map path until it is retired.\n1931: \n1932: ### 9.2 The observer lane: an integration item to scope, not a solved detail\n1933: \n1934: Program input reaches an evaluator through\n1935: `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:217-247`),\n1936: which is shape-agnostic: it hands each observed batch to a\n1937: composition-supplied `load`. The per-evaluator loaders are chosen in\n1938: `yin.repl.core/program-loaders` (`repl/v2/core.cljc:63-68`) and today\n1939: accept datom batches; the REPL's own eval path still converts a\n1940: semantic-VM AST through `ast->datoms` before it travels\n1941: (`repl/v2/core.cljc:444-449`). Two things about the row lane are\n1942: **unverified**:\n1943: \n1944: - **The batch shape.** Whether one batch is one tree's row set, one\n1945:   `{:yin/code :yin/declarations}` value (§8.5), one vector, or a\n1946:   collection of them is\n1947:   a medium contract this design has not exercised end to end; §7.1 assumes\n1948:   one canonical value per batch. The shape must be fixed with the REPL\n1949:   shell and the `yin.vm.stream-observer` tests before the loaders are\n1950:   switched.\n1951: - **The program-input predicate.** `engine/executable-program-datom?`\n1952:   (`engine.cljc:511-513`) decides what counts as program input by testing\n1953:   the attribute namespace of an `[e a v t m]` row, and\n1954:   `append-program-datoms` (`engine.cljc:590-612`) builds on it. A row\n1955:   batch has no attribute slot. A repository search on 2026-09-14 found no\n1956:   caller of the v2 `append-program-datoms` beyond its own overload, so a\n1957:   row counterpart is not presumed necessary; whether any live\n1958:   composition reaches the predicate is what the implementation phase\n1959:   establishes before deciding.\n1960: \n1961: Both are integration items to scope in the implementation phase.\n1962: \n1963: ### 9.3 What stays on datoms, and why\n1964: \n1965: This is §1's datom-layer jobs\n1966: seen from the migration side:\n1967: \n1968: - **The ledger, every ref, and every ledger event** (§8): mutability,\n1969:   history, provenance, and custody are datom properties, and the\n1970:   transactor is what possesses them.\n1971: - **`dao.space.index` and published covered indexes**: they index datoms,\n1972:   and the scale path of §6.5 goes through them.\n1973: - **The macro expander's event log** (§8.4): events are entities with\n1974:   attributes, one per attempt; only their values changed kind.\n1975: - **The datom projection of code** (§6.5): kept for the three query classes\n1976:   it alone serves, regenerable from the content at any time.\n1977: - **Execution traces** (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.6): state over time is a\n1978:   datom stream by design and is untouched.\n1979: \n1980: ---\n1981: \n1982: ## §10 Acceptance blockers and open items\n1983: \n1984: 1. **The pinned canonical byte encoding in `dao.jing`** — declared and\n1985:    inherited, still open, but **no longer blocking ordinary identity and\n1986:    deduplication use** (§4.2). The three structural conformance pairs\n1987:    this item used to name as blocking (vector/list, vector/seq,\n1988:    collection metadata) are closed by `dao.jing`'s committed encoder fix\n1989:    — list and seq intentionally remain one address, since `=` calls them\n1990:    equal. What\n1991:    remains is the transitional hand printer's disclosed residuals\n1992:    (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte-array identity hashing,\n1993:    ambient print-var bindings on scalars — `dao.jing.md`'s Open Items)\n1994:    and the eventual pinned byte encoding itself, neither of which this\n1995:    design closes.\n1996: 2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,\n1997:    extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one\n1998:    definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation\n1999:    phase must not reintroduce a narrower one, and the corpus must contain\n2000:    a numeric key beside the keyword one.\n2001: 3. **Complete occurrence identity** (§2.5, §5.3, §8.4) — design work\n2002:    with stated mechanisms, not yet implementable. Source origins\n2003:    `[:source medium batch j]` must be supplied to the lowering and\n2004:    recorded in instruction provenance and initial expansion events;\n2005:    nested expansion events must carry both the declared-ref parent link\n2006:    and the parent record address, and the record must be minted after\n2007:    the parent's. The medium identity coordinate is whatever the\n2008:    composition names its program medium by; this document does not fix\n2009:    its shape, and that shape must be fixed before an origin can be\n2010:    written. The expander incarnation (§8.4.1) is a composition-minted\n2011:    random token, never a log-allocated id or a stream position; its\n2012:    uniqueness is the composition's warranty, discharged by the token's\n2013:    randomness, and no transactor is claimed to provide it. The\n2014:    `:incarnation` field of `ctx` and the token-minting step at\n2015:    construction are additions to `yin.vm.macro.md`'s `ctx` and expander\n2016:    construction that this document cannot make there.\n2017: 4. **Macro batch and admission preservation** (§8.5) — design work with\n2018:    stated mechanisms. The ordered batch, the admission-order harvest\n2019:    catalogue of occurrence groups, the derived macro-only declaration\n2020:    catalogue, its admission validation, the validate-before-strip rule,\n2021:    and the adapter's path-and-entity tracking during inlining must exist\n2022:    before any tuple program containing a macro definition can be\n2023:    admitted. The legacy datom-batch contract is\n2024:    preserved only through the adapter-derived catalogue; tuple-native\n2025:    frontends operate\n2026:    under the separate declaration-order contract stated there. The v2\n2027:    expander itself does not exist yet (`src/cljc/yin/vm/` has no\n2028:    `macro.cljc`) and is not a blocker for tuple evaluation without\n2029:    macros; §8.4's event shape is specified against `yin.vm.macro.md`, not\n2030:    against code.\n2031: 5. **Conservative dependency completion** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3) — design\n2032:    work with stated mechanisms. Free-name discharge requires searching the\n2033:    store slice by key or resolving via primitive/module profiles (§7.7.2);\n2034:    convergence is over work items and all dependency facts, with the\n2035:    captured-environment context abstraction for values. Neither analysis exists in code;\n2036:    until they do, an emitter may only report `:incomplete`. Extracting the\n2037:    free-name set itself must use an occurrence-joined query, not a\n2038:    row-only one, to stay conservative when a name is free at one\n2039:    occurrence and bound at another (§4.5).\n2040: 6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be\n2041:    published with the execution-contract stamp, and the tree/segment\n2042:    requirement-set equality must be tested over the corpus. FFI ops are a\n2043:    receiver capability requirement, never a store-slice requirement.\n2044: 7. **The lowering profile** (§5.2.1): `\"ast-v1\"` must be published as a\n2045:    pinned document before any derivation record is written; until then\n2046:    no derivation can be verified, only content integrity (§5.2.2).\n2047: 8. **UCF §7.6.1 needs two amendments** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3): its\n2048:    any-carried-environment satisfaction sentence is unsound and must be\n2049:    replaced by the store-slice-by-key / profile rule of §7.7.2 (an environment never\n2050:    discharges a name), and its address-per-segment fixed\n2051:    point must become the work-item fixed point. This document cannot edit\n2052:    UCF.\n2053: 9. **The observer-lane batch shape and the program-input predicate**\n2054:    (§9.2) — integration items with no design answer in this document. The\n2055:    §8.5 ordered batch is a candidate shape for the AST medium, not a\n2056:    verified one.\n2057: 10. **Ledger event representation on the transactor** (§8.2): the\n2058:     event-entity form is legal under `local-datom?` as written, but the\n2059:     map-`m` provenance path (`transact.cljc:15-29`) and the metadata-op\n2060:     interpreter `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names are not exercised by\n2061:     this design's tests yet. The naming rows need neither; the provenance\n2062:     link from a naming fact to its event does.\n2063: 11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every\n2064:     retained name obligation is checked by profile under UCF §7.5.2, and\n2065:     §7.7.2 reads callable effects from profiles; until\n2066:     `yin.vm/primitives` (`v2.cljc:100-132`) is published with profiles,\n2067:     dependency closure reports names and `:incomplete`, not satisfiable\n2068:     bindings.\n2069: 12. **Per-lambda subterm addressing** — dissolved by the flat-row form\n2070:     (§4.3): every node is addressed by construction, and the remaining\n2071:     one-segment-per-lambda lowering question is a §5.2.1 profile concern.\n2072: 13. **The round-trip law** (§7.2, part 1) — a first-class conformance\n2073:     obligation, not a blocker: for every corpus program, `map → rows →\n2074:     map` is the identity on the canonical map AST and `rows → map →\n2075:     rows` is the identity on the rows, and both directions must be\n2076:     tested over the corpus before any row set is treated as canonical.\n2077:     The corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, so the test\n2078:     is writable now.\n2079: 14. **`yin.vm.semantic.md` Instruction Grammar Amendment** (§5.1) — the `ast-v1` lowering\n2080:     profile (§5.2.1) pins the §5.3 table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, which is currently\n2081:     the name-based one. That document's §2.4 and §5.3 must be amended to add\n2082:     the argument-to-named-parameter binding rule of §7.7.2 (arguments match `params` by position, each bound to its name; under-arity call → missing parameter names bound to `nil`; extra arguments beyond the params list length are dropped)\n2083:     before any derivation record is written.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
