))\n    68\t          (vector? n)\n    69\t          (prefixed\n    70\t            (str \"[\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \"]\"))\n    71\t          (sequential? n)\n    72\t          (prefixed\n    73\t            (str \"(\" (str/join \" \" (map canonical-print n)) \")\"))\n    74\t          :else (pr-str n))))\n    75\t\n    46\t(defn resolve-var\n    47\t  \"Look up a variable name: env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n    48\t\n    49\t   The registry is a value supplied by the composition, not a global.\"\n    50\t  [env store primitives registry name]\n    51\t  (if-let [pair (find env name)]\n    52\t    (val pair)\n    53\t    (if-let [pair (find store name)]\n    54\t      (val pair)\n    55\t      (if-let [pair (find primitives name)]\n    56\t        (val pair)\n    57\t        (if-let [resolved (when (namespace name)\n    58\t                            (module/resolve-module\n    59\t                              registry\n    60\t                              (symbol (str (namespace name)\n    61\t                                           \".\"\n    62\t                                           (clojure.core/name name)))))]\n    63\t          resolved\n    64\t          (fail (str \"Unable to resolve symbol: \" name \" in this context\")\n    65\t                {:symbol name}))))))\n156:The fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the `:lambda` arm already binds `params` by name, and the `:variable` arm already resolves through `resolve-var`'s env → store → primitives → modules fallthrough (`ast_walker.cljc:361-363`; the semantic VM's `:var` opcode does the same at `semantic.cljc:256-259`) — this design keeps that fallthrough rather than splitting free and bound references into separate node types (§4.5). Separately, closure application (`ast_walker.cljc:191`, and its hot-path copies at `:511`/`:553`; `semantic.cljc:200`) still binds arguments with `(zipmap params args)`, which leaves missing parameter names unbound rather than bound to `nil` — that binding change belongs to the application arm and the semantic VM's apply, not to `:lambda`.\n633:**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective over ordinary values, which it now is.** The three conformance pairs (list/vector, seq/list, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal. The disclosed residuals (scalar metadata, pathological symbols, byte-array identity hashing) remain gaps in the transitional encoder, but none of them are reachable through this collapse for ordinary values — see §4.2.\n649:params, resolved against the lexical environment only, and `:global` for\n663:a `:global`-shaped fact, derived on demand, never persisted. `dao.space.query/q`\n671:`store` a `:global` reference resolves into is genuinely mutable and\n675:resolution, evaluating an unbound name to `nil` rather than falling\n680:frontend scope-analysis work (classifying free names as `:global` at\n690:a row tree with no `:global` tag anywhere in it, including the\n702:**The row-only query is unsound, not just imprecise, when a name is free\n704:cost of dropping `:global`, only of the simple query.** Because\n715:imprecision. The row-only query can undercount in this mixed case, so it\n718:The fix is not to bring `:global` back. A name's free/bound status at a\n730:the exercise — a **contrast assertion that runs the row-only rule against\n736:**Not root-scoped as written — a real gap, not a hedge.** The test's\n750:and outperforms the row-only rule even across the whole §2.3 grammar\n752:share the row-only rule's separate gap of only knowing `:lambda`'s and\n753:`:application`'s node-valued slots — see the row-only rule's own\n755:root-scoping the rules and building the indexer that emits occurrence\n1163:There is no vector-level binding rule for `:var`: nesting is a property of the AST, checked by §